1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,277
Welcome to episode fourteen, our
second and final exam workshop.

2
00:00:04,317 --> 00:00:09,134
We'll work through two longer practice
questions on calorimetry and data

3
00:00:09,174 --> 00:00:11,899
evaluation, recap how to choose a method,

4
00:00:11,939 --> 00:00:15,946
and finish with a quick diagnostic
on the most common traps.

5
00:00:15,986 --> 00:00:20,129
These are original practice
questions written for this series,

6
00:00:20,169 --> 00:00:22,057
not official exam questions.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,469
Two retrieval checks first.

8
00:00:24,509 --> 00:00:30,876
How many moles of hydrochloric acid are in
twenty-five point zero millilitres of a

9
00:00:30,916 --> 00:00:34,704
zero point two zero zero
mole per litre solution?

10
00:00:34,744 --> 00:00:41,189
And a heater runs at six point zero zero
volts and one point five zero amps for two

11
00:00:41,229 --> 00:00:43,064
point zero zero minutes.

12
00:00:43,104 --> 00:00:46,346
How much electrical energy does it supply?

13
00:00:46,386 --> 00:00:47,714
Pause and answer.

14
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:02,001
For the acid, convert the
volume to litres first:

15
00:01:02,041 --> 00:01:08,301
zero point two zero zero times zero point
zero two five zero is zero point zero

16
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:10,374
zero five zero zero moles.

17
00:01:10,414 --> 00:01:13,883
For the heater, convert
the time to seconds:

18
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:20,342
six point zero zero times one point five
zero times one hundred and twenty is one

19
00:01:20,382 --> 00:01:22,495
thousand and eighty joules.

20
00:01:22,535 --> 00:01:24,688
Units first, then multiply.

21
00:01:25,666 --> 00:01:29,356
Practice question Q twenty-seven
is worth fourteen marks.

22
00:01:29,396 --> 00:01:31,842
It combines an electrical calibration,

23
00:01:31,882 --> 00:01:35,114
a neutralisation reaction
and a temperature graph.

24
00:01:35,154 --> 00:01:37,379
Read it all the way through first.

25
00:01:37,733 --> 00:01:41,460
Pause the video now and
attempt every part on paper.

26
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:44,792
Give yourself about
fifteen to twenty minutes.

27
00:01:44,832 --> 00:01:49,863
If you keep the video running, the
next minute is quiet thinking time:

28
00:01:49,903 --> 00:01:54,177
sketch the temperature graph
before you calculate anything.

29
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:56,315
Let's annotate.

30
00:02:56,355 --> 00:03:02,397
The calibration uses five point zero zero
volts and one point two zero amps for

31
00:03:02,437 --> 00:03:07,786
three hundred seconds, giving a rise
of three point zero zero degrees.

32
00:03:07,826 --> 00:03:13,021
That's E equals V times I times t,
with the time already in seconds,

33
00:03:13,061 --> 00:03:15,602
followed by a calibration factor.

34
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,241
The question says the calibration
factor applies to the reaction mixture.

35
00:03:21,281 --> 00:03:27,246
So for the reaction, the heat is the
calibration factor times the temperature rise.

36
00:03:27,286 --> 00:03:30,285
We won't use m c delta T for the reaction,

37
00:03:30,325 --> 00:03:36,402
because the calibration already accounts
for the water, the cup and the thermometer.

38
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:42,046
The solutions: fifty point zero millilitres
of one point zero zero molar acid,

39
00:03:42,086 --> 00:03:47,602
and seventy-five point zero millilitres of
zero point eight zero zero molar sodium

40
00:03:47,642 --> 00:03:48,280
hydroxide.

41
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:53,064
Convert millilitres to litres, and
expect one reactant to be limiting.

42
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:54,747
Finally, the graph.

43
00:03:54,787 --> 00:04:00,226
The baseline is twenty point zero
degrees, mixing happens at sixty seconds,

44
00:04:00,266 --> 00:04:04,973
and the cooling readings start at
one hundred and twenty seconds.

45
00:04:05,013 --> 00:04:10,491
So we'll need to extrapolate the cooling
line back to the moment of mixing.

46
00:04:11,466 --> 00:04:14,001
Plan the chain before calculating.

47
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:18,847
Part a: E equals V times I times
t, then the calibration factor,

48
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:21,800
then a comparison with the water alone.

49
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:26,570
Part b: moles of each solution
from concentration times volume,

50
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:28,615
then the limiting reactant.

51
00:04:28,655 --> 00:04:32,704
Part c: the corrected
temperature rise from the graph.

52
00:04:32,744 --> 00:04:37,853
Part d: the heat from the calibration
factor and the corrected rise,

53
00:04:37,893 --> 00:04:41,224
divided by the limiting
amount, with a sign.

54
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:44,449
Parts a, b and c all feed part d,

55
00:04:44,489 --> 00:04:49,616
so an error in any of them
carries through to the enthalpy.

56
00:04:49,656 --> 00:04:52,594
Parts e and f are reasoning parts:

57
00:04:52,634 --> 00:04:57,450
they test whether you understand
what the numbers mean.

58
00:04:58,399 --> 00:04:58,866
Part a.

59
00:04:58,906 --> 00:05:04,873
The calibration energy is five point zero
zero times one point two zero times three

60
00:05:04,913 --> 00:05:07,985
hundred: one thousand
eight hundred joules.

61
00:05:08,025 --> 00:05:13,920
The calibration factor is one thousand
eight hundred joules divided by three point

62
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:17,506
zero zero degrees: six
hundred joules per degree.

63
00:05:17,866 --> 00:05:19,586
Now a plausibility check.

64
00:05:19,626 --> 00:05:25,360
The water alone would need one hundred and
twenty-five point zero grams times four

65
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:30,571
point one eight: five hundred and
twenty-two point five joules per degree.

66
00:05:30,611 --> 00:05:35,430
The calibration factor is larger,
which makes sense, because the cup,

67
00:05:35,470 --> 00:05:39,302
the thermometer and the
stirrer absorb some energy too.

68
00:05:39,342 --> 00:05:44,443
A calibration factor smaller than the
water alone would suggest an error.

69
00:05:44,483 --> 00:05:48,919
Three marks: the energy, the
calibration factor, and the check.

70
00:05:49,866 --> 00:05:50,380
Part b.

71
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:56,872
Hydrochloric acid: one point zero zero
times zero point zero five zero zero litres

72
00:05:56,912 --> 00:06:00,039
is zero point zero five zero zero moles.

73
00:06:00,079 --> 00:06:06,532
Sodium hydroxide: zero point eight zero
zero times zero point zero seven five zero

74
00:06:06,572 --> 00:06:10,214
litres is zero point zero
six zero zero moles.

75
00:06:10,599 --> 00:06:13,521
They react one to one, forming water.

76
00:06:13,561 --> 00:06:18,806
So the acid, at zero point zero
five zero zero moles, is limiting,

77
00:06:18,846 --> 00:06:24,650
and zero point zero one zero zero
moles of sodium hydroxide is left over.

78
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:30,895
The heat released depends on the zero
point zero five zero zero moles of water

79
00:06:30,935 --> 00:06:31,456
formed.

80
00:06:31,496 --> 00:06:35,499
Two marks: the amounts,
and the limiting reactant.

81
00:06:36,465 --> 00:06:36,902
Part c.

82
00:06:36,942 --> 00:06:37,857
Plot the data.

83
00:06:37,897 --> 00:06:42,766
The baseline is twenty point zero
degrees until mixing at sixty seconds.

84
00:06:42,806 --> 00:06:47,130
After that, the readings fall
steadily: twenty-four point seven,

85
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:52,654
twenty-four point six, twenty-four point
five and twenty-four point four degrees,

86
00:06:52,694 --> 00:06:54,330
one every sixty seconds.

87
00:06:54,665 --> 00:06:58,676
The highest reading is
twenty-four point seven degrees,

88
00:06:58,716 --> 00:07:02,579
but by then the mixture had
already been losing heat.

89
00:07:02,619 --> 00:07:06,851
So draw the best straight line
through the cooling points,

90
00:07:06,891 --> 00:07:11,163
and extend it back to sixty
seconds, the moment of mixing.

91
00:07:11,531 --> 00:07:15,762
The line falls by zero point
one degrees every sixty seconds,

92
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:20,172
so at sixty seconds it reaches
twenty-four point eight degrees.

93
00:07:20,212 --> 00:07:25,913
The corrected temperature rise is twenty-four
point eight minus twenty point zero:

94
00:07:25,953 --> 00:07:27,663
four point eight degrees.

95
00:07:27,703 --> 00:07:32,114
One mark for the extrapolation,
and one for the corrected rise.

96
00:07:33,064 --> 00:07:33,530
Part d.

97
00:07:33,570 --> 00:07:39,457
The heat absorbed by the calorimeter is
the calibration factor times the corrected

98
00:07:39,497 --> 00:07:42,421
rise: six hundred times four point eight,

99
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:46,324
which is two thousand eight
hundred and eighty joules,

100
00:07:46,364 --> 00:07:49,328
or two point eight eight zero kilojoules.

101
00:07:49,664 --> 00:07:52,827
Divide by the amount of
the limiting reactant,

102
00:07:52,867 --> 00:07:55,612
which equals the amount of water formed:

103
00:07:55,652 --> 00:08:01,112
two point eight eight zero kilojoules
divided by zero point zero five zero zero

104
00:08:01,152 --> 00:08:03,271
moles is fifty-seven point six.

105
00:08:03,311 --> 00:08:08,284
The reaction released heat into the
calorimeter, so delta H is negative:

106
00:08:08,324 --> 00:08:13,923
negative fifty-seven point six, or about
negative fifty-eight kilojoules per mole

107
00:08:13,963 --> 00:08:14,550
of water.

108
00:08:14,590 --> 00:08:19,116
Two significant figures, because
the corrected rise has only two.

109
00:08:19,464 --> 00:08:24,134
Three marks: the heat, the
division by the limiting amount,

110
00:08:24,174 --> 00:08:27,686
and delta H with the
correct sign and units.

111
00:08:28,664 --> 00:08:31,284
Here are three tempting wrong answers.

112
00:08:31,324 --> 00:08:36,813
The first divides by zero point zero six
zero zero moles, the sodium hydroxide,

113
00:08:36,853 --> 00:08:41,083
and gets negative forty-eight
point zero kilojoules per mole.

114
00:08:41,123 --> 00:08:46,583
But the excess sodium hydroxide never
reacted, so it can't be the denominator.

115
00:08:46,930 --> 00:08:51,344
The second uses the highest reading,
twenty-four point seven degrees,

116
00:08:51,384 --> 00:08:53,538
instead of the extrapolated value.

117
00:08:53,578 --> 00:08:56,249
The rise becomes four point seven degrees,

118
00:08:56,289 --> 00:08:59,476
and delta H becomes negative
fifty-six point four.

119
00:08:59,516 --> 00:09:03,195
It's an underestimate, because
cooling had already begun.

120
00:09:03,530 --> 00:09:08,815
The third ignores the calibration and
uses m c delta T with one hundred and

121
00:09:08,855 --> 00:09:10,945
twenty-five grams of solution.

122
00:09:10,985 --> 00:09:15,205
That gives about two thousand
five hundred and eight joules,

123
00:09:15,245 --> 00:09:19,109
and about negative fifty
point two kilojoules per mole.

124
00:09:19,149 --> 00:09:23,369
It leaves out the energy absorbed
by the calorimeter itself,

125
00:09:23,409 --> 00:09:27,243
which is exactly what the
calibration factor includes.

126
00:09:28,197 --> 00:09:28,692
Part e.

127
00:09:28,732 --> 00:09:32,891
If repeats of this experiment
gave very similar values,

128
00:09:32,931 --> 00:09:36,938
that would show the result
is repeatable, or precise.

129
00:09:36,978 --> 00:09:39,763
It would not show that it's accurate.

130
00:09:39,803 --> 00:09:45,872
A systematic error, such as a wrong
calibration factor or unaccounted heat loss,

131
00:09:45,912 --> 00:09:49,232
would shift every repeat
by the same amount.

132
00:09:49,272 --> 00:09:51,028
One mark for each idea.

133
00:09:51,397 --> 00:09:51,905
Part f.

134
00:09:51,945 --> 00:09:58,323
Doubling the sodium hydroxide concentration
gives zero point one two zero moles of

135
00:09:58,363 --> 00:09:58,558
it.

136
00:09:58,598 --> 00:10:03,567
But the acid is still only zero
point zero five zero zero moles,

137
00:10:03,607 --> 00:10:05,445
and it's still limiting.

138
00:10:05,485 --> 00:10:10,220
The same amount of water forms,
so the same heat is released.

139
00:10:10,260 --> 00:10:14,408
Two marks: the limiting
reactant, and the conclusion.

140
00:10:14,797 --> 00:10:20,703
Here's the full tally for Q twenty-seven:
three, two, two, three, two and two,

141
00:10:20,743 --> 00:10:22,192
for fourteen marks.

142
00:10:23,129 --> 00:10:25,030
Checkpoint: spot the error.

143
00:10:25,070 --> 00:10:30,563
Here are three lines from different
students' answers to Q twenty-seven and Q

144
00:10:30,603 --> 00:10:31,497
twenty-eight.

145
00:10:31,537 --> 00:10:33,365
Each contains one mistake.

146
00:10:33,405 --> 00:10:35,202
Pause and find all three.

147
00:10:50,663 --> 00:10:54,452
Line one forgets to convert
millilitres to litres.

148
00:10:54,492 --> 00:10:59,813
Fifty point zero millilitres is zero
point zero five zero zero litres,

149
00:10:59,853 --> 00:11:04,678
so the acid is zero point zero
five zero zero moles, not fifty.

150
00:11:05,063 --> 00:11:07,012
Line two has the wrong sign.

151
00:11:07,052 --> 00:11:12,697
The temperature rose because the reaction
released heat, so delta H is negative:

152
00:11:12,737 --> 00:11:16,361
negative fifty-seven point
six kilojoules per mole.

153
00:11:16,729 --> 00:11:20,316
Line three divides joules
by kilojoules per mole.

154
00:11:20,356 --> 00:11:25,422
Nine hundred and sixty joules is
zero point nine six zero kilojoules,

155
00:11:25,462 --> 00:11:28,679
giving zero point zero
four zero zero moles.

156
00:11:28,719 --> 00:11:34,155
The wrong value would make the mass of
X three thousand two hundred grams,

157
00:11:34,195 --> 00:11:37,189
far more than the whole four gram sample,

158
00:11:37,229 --> 00:11:40,856
which a plausibility check
catches straight away.

159
00:11:41,795 --> 00:11:45,373
Practice question Q twenty-eight
is worth twelve marks.

160
00:11:45,413 --> 00:11:49,976
It tests a manufacturer's claim about
an idealised hand-warmer powder.

161
00:11:50,016 --> 00:11:54,251
The active solid, X, is hypothetical,
invented for this question.

162
00:11:54,291 --> 00:11:55,343
Read it through.

163
00:11:55,729 --> 00:11:59,291
Pause the video now and
attempt all six parts.

164
00:11:59,331 --> 00:12:03,676
If you keep it running, here's
some quiet thinking time.

165
00:12:03,716 --> 00:12:09,510
Start by deciding what the temperature
rise tells you, and in which units.

166
00:12:55,529 --> 00:12:56,034
Part a.

167
00:12:56,074 --> 00:13:01,951
The heat gained by the calorimeter is
the calibration factor times the rise:

168
00:13:01,991 --> 00:13:08,258
three hundred times three point two zero,
which is nine hundred and sixty joules.

169
00:13:08,298 --> 00:13:09,098
Two marks.

170
00:13:09,462 --> 00:13:09,943
Part b.

171
00:13:09,983 --> 00:13:15,000
Dissolving one mole of X releases
twenty-four point zero kilojoules.

172
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:20,951
So the amount of X is zero point nine six
zero kilojoules divided by twenty-four

173
00:13:20,991 --> 00:13:26,083
point zero kilojoules per mole: zero
point zero four zero zero moles.

174
00:13:26,123 --> 00:13:29,990
Watch the units: convert
joules to kilojoules first.

175
00:13:30,329 --> 00:13:37,005
The mass of X is zero point zero four zero
zero moles times eighty point zero grams

176
00:13:37,045 --> 00:13:40,000
per mole: three point two zero grams.

177
00:13:40,040 --> 00:13:44,531
As a percentage of the four
point zero zero gram sample,

178
00:13:44,571 --> 00:13:47,202
that's eighty point zero percent.

179
00:13:47,242 --> 00:13:49,669
Four marks across those steps.

180
00:13:50,062 --> 00:13:50,544
Part c.

181
00:13:50,584 --> 00:13:56,732
Eighty point zero percent is well below
the claimed ninety-five point zero percent,

182
00:13:56,772 --> 00:14:00,236
so this measurement does
not support the claim.

183
00:14:00,276 --> 00:14:01,076
One mark.

184
00:14:01,927 --> 00:14:02,369
Part d.

185
00:14:02,409 --> 00:14:07,799
Suppose a spreadsheet mistakenly uses a
calibration factor of three hundred and

186
00:14:07,839 --> 00:14:11,099
sixty joules per degree,
from a different setup.

187
00:14:11,139 --> 00:14:15,429
The heat becomes one thousand
one hundred and fifty-two joules,

188
00:14:15,469 --> 00:14:19,347
the amount becomes zero point
zero four eight zero moles,

189
00:14:19,387 --> 00:14:22,509
the mass becomes three
point eight four grams,

190
00:14:22,549 --> 00:14:26,467
and the percentage becomes
ninety-six point zero percent.

191
00:14:26,861 --> 00:14:29,356
Follow the direction of the error.

192
00:14:29,396 --> 00:14:35,397
A calibration factor that's too large
makes every quantity calculated from it too

193
00:14:35,437 --> 00:14:35,845
large.

194
00:14:35,885 --> 00:14:40,543
Here, it makes a powder that
fails the claim appear to meet it.

195
00:14:40,583 --> 00:14:44,312
Two marks: the recalculation,
and the implication.

196
00:14:44,661 --> 00:14:45,147
Part e.

197
00:14:45,187 --> 00:14:50,639
Repeating the test with the same wrong
calibration factor won't fix this.

198
00:14:50,679 --> 00:14:53,196
Each repeat carries the same bias,

199
00:14:53,236 --> 00:14:57,258
so the results would agree
closely and still be wrong.

200
00:14:57,298 --> 00:15:00,568
Precision doesn't remove
a systematic error.

201
00:15:00,608 --> 00:15:01,408
One mark.

202
00:15:02,260 --> 00:15:02,813
Part f.

203
00:15:02,853 --> 00:15:09,755
Suppose the powder absorbs moisture from
the air before a new four point zero zero

204
00:15:09,795 --> 00:15:11,702
gram sample is weighed.

205
00:15:11,742 --> 00:15:16,189
Some of that four point zero
zero grams is now water,

206
00:15:16,229 --> 00:15:18,938
so there's less X in the sample.

207
00:15:19,327 --> 00:15:25,118
Less X dissolves, so less heat is
released, the temperature rise is smaller,

208
00:15:25,158 --> 00:15:28,495
and the calculated
percentage of X is lower.

209
00:15:28,535 --> 00:15:33,522
Two marks: the reduced active
mass, and its effect on the result.

210
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:39,405
The full tally for Q twenty-eight:
two, four, one, two, one and two,

211
00:15:39,445 --> 00:15:40,841
for twelve marks.

212
00:15:41,792 --> 00:15:45,457
One more checkpoint, on
the direction of errors.

213
00:15:45,497 --> 00:15:48,776
For each change to the
Q twenty-eight test,

214
00:15:48,816 --> 00:15:54,565
decide whether the calculated percentage
of X would be too high or too low.

215
00:15:54,605 --> 00:15:58,887
First, heat is lost to the
surroundings during the test.

216
00:15:58,927 --> 00:16:04,984
Second, the sample mass is recorded as
three point eight zero grams when it was

217
00:16:05,024 --> 00:16:07,608
really four point zero zero grams.

218
00:16:07,648 --> 00:16:11,313
Third, the calibration
factor used is too small.

219
00:16:11,353 --> 00:16:12,665
Pause and decide.

220
00:16:25,125 --> 00:16:31,179
Heat loss makes the measured rise too
small, so the percentage comes out too low.

221
00:16:31,219 --> 00:16:37,197
Recording a smaller sample mass than the
real one makes the percentage too high:

222
00:16:37,237 --> 00:16:43,291
three point two zero divided by three
point eight zero is about eighty-four point

223
00:16:43,331 --> 00:16:44,193
two percent.

224
00:16:44,233 --> 00:16:50,212
And a calibration factor that's too small
makes the heat, and so the percentage,

225
00:16:50,252 --> 00:16:50,814
too low.

226
00:16:50,854 --> 00:16:55,819
In each case, follow the change
through the chain of calculations.

227
00:16:56,792 --> 00:17:01,267
Let's recap how to choose a
method across the whole topic.

228
00:17:01,307 --> 00:17:03,525
Start from what you're given.

229
00:17:03,565 --> 00:17:07,340
A mass gives moles by
dividing by the molar mass.

230
00:17:07,380 --> 00:17:12,867
A gas volume at SLC gives moles by
dividing by twenty-four point eight.

231
00:17:12,907 --> 00:17:17,968
A solution gives moles from
concentration times volume in litres.

232
00:17:18,325 --> 00:17:24,510
For energy: the moles of fuel times the
size of delta H gives the energy released.

233
00:17:24,550 --> 00:17:26,864
Heating water uses m c delta T.

234
00:17:26,904 --> 00:17:30,583
An electrical calibration
uses V times I times t,

235
00:17:30,623 --> 00:17:36,201
and the calibration factor is that
energy divided by the temperature rise.

236
00:17:36,241 --> 00:17:41,895
With a calibrated calorimeter, the heat
is the calibration factor times the

237
00:17:41,935 --> 00:17:43,225
temperature rise.

238
00:17:43,592 --> 00:17:48,987
Molar enthalpy is the heat divided by
the moles of the limiting reactant,

239
00:17:49,027 --> 00:17:52,784
with a negative sign if
the reaction released heat.

240
00:17:52,824 --> 00:17:55,688
Efficiency is useful output over input.

241
00:17:55,728 --> 00:18:00,974
To find the input needed for a target
output, divide by the efficiency.

242
00:18:01,014 --> 00:18:04,588
And whenever you compare
fuels, state the basis.

243
00:18:05,525 --> 00:18:07,337
Now a quick diagnostic.

244
00:18:07,377 --> 00:18:09,269
Here are six statements.

245
00:18:09,309 --> 00:18:13,939
Decide whether each is correct,
and if not, name the trap.

246
00:18:13,979 --> 00:18:15,348
Pause and decide.

247
00:18:40,792 --> 00:18:41,577
One: wrong.

248
00:18:41,617 --> 00:18:45,632
Convert seventy-five point
zero millilitres to litres,

249
00:18:45,672 --> 00:18:48,861
giving zero point zero
six zero zero moles.

250
00:18:48,901 --> 00:18:49,686
Two: wrong.

251
00:18:49,726 --> 00:18:53,816
Use only the reacting
component's share of the mixture.

252
00:18:53,856 --> 00:18:54,792
Three: wrong.

253
00:18:54,832 --> 00:18:57,871
Only twenty-one percent of air is oxygen.

254
00:18:57,911 --> 00:18:58,772
Four: wrong.

255
00:18:58,812 --> 00:19:03,427
Divide by the limiting reactant,
the one that actually reacts,

256
00:19:03,467 --> 00:19:05,079
not the one in excess.

257
00:19:05,119 --> 00:19:05,980
Five: wrong.

258
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:10,560
Joules per degree times degrees
gives joules, not kilojoules.

259
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:11,576
Six: correct.

260
00:19:11,925 --> 00:19:12,726
Six more.

261
00:19:12,766 --> 00:19:14,354
Pause and decide.

262
00:19:39,792 --> 00:19:40,744
Seven: wrong.

263
00:19:40,784 --> 00:19:45,936
The highest reading underestimates
the rise when heat is being lost,

264
00:19:45,976 --> 00:19:48,379
so extrapolate the cooling line.

265
00:19:48,419 --> 00:19:49,371
Eight: wrong.

266
00:19:49,411 --> 00:19:55,403
Close agreement shows precision, but a
systematic error can shift every repeat.

267
00:19:55,443 --> 00:19:56,319
Nine: wrong.

268
00:19:56,359 --> 00:20:01,434
A catalyst lowers the activation
energy but doesn't change delta H.

269
00:20:01,474 --> 00:20:02,427
Ten: correct.

270
00:20:02,467 --> 00:20:03,496
Eleven: wrong.

271
00:20:03,536 --> 00:20:06,855
Per mole of fuel isn't
per useful kilojoule;

272
00:20:06,895 --> 00:20:10,672
the energy released and
the efficiency matter too.

273
00:20:10,712 --> 00:20:11,741
Twelve: wrong.

274
00:20:11,781 --> 00:20:18,002
A renewable fuel can still compete with
food, use a lot of water, or leak methane.

275
00:20:18,042 --> 00:20:20,867
Add any you missed to your error log.

276
00:20:21,858 --> 00:20:23,691
That's the end of the series.

277
00:20:23,731 --> 00:20:27,436
You now have a full toolkit:
mole calculations with units,

278
00:20:27,476 --> 00:20:32,796
energy from reactions and fuels, calorimetry
with calibration and graph correction,

279
00:20:32,836 --> 00:20:35,226
and fair, evidence-based comparisons.

280
00:20:35,592 --> 00:20:41,135
Use the worksheet to attempt every
practice question again without the video,

281
00:20:41,175 --> 00:20:45,703
then check the worked solutions,
including the marks and traps.

282
00:20:45,743 --> 00:20:51,504
Keep the formula and method sheet beside
you, and keep adding to your error log.

283
00:20:51,544 --> 00:20:52,344
Good luck.
